*Why max-aggregation at the message-passing level?* Day 1 showed that {1,3}-RU mixed augmentation with mean-aggregation MP + max-pooling readout still failed — the conflicting signals are averaged away *before* they reach the readout. Max-aggregation in the MP layers means each node selects the strongest neighbor signal per feature dimension. When a node receives messages from both its 1-RU and 3-RU representations, max-aggregation preserves the most structurally informative signal rather than averaging them into mush. This is the GRIN insight.

*Why learnable structural encodings with k≥2?* In polymers, the backbone connectivity creates a characteristic topology: atom i connects to i+1, i-1 (k=1), and also has distinctive k=2 patterns (next-nearest neighbors along the backbone vs. across a ring). Static encodings treat all k=2 connections identically. A learnable MLP over adjacency powers can learn that "k=2 along the backbone" differs from "k=2 across an aromatic ring." This is polymer-specific inductive bias.

*Interaction:* Max-aggregation's selectivity amplifies the benefit of better node features — if LSE helps distinguish backbone atoms from side-chain atoms, max-aggregation will preferentially route information along the backbone, which is exactly what Tg prediction needs (backbone rigidity is the dominant physical determinant).

