## Day 3 Paper Selection: Self-Supervised Pretraining for Polymers

### From Pool (2 papers)

| # | Paper | Justification |
|---|-------|---------------|
| 1 | **JEPA Polymer** (2025) — *Joint Embedding Predictive Architecture for self-supervised pretraining on polymer molecular graphs* | **Direct hit**: Applies JEPA-based SSL directly to polymer molecular graphs. Tests whether pretraining improves downstream property prediction with scarce labels — precisely our PI1M → Tg scenario. Provides a ready-to-adapt SSL framework on the exact data modality we work with. |
| 2 | **KPGT** (2022) — *Knowledge-Guided Pre-training of Graph Transformer for Molecular Property Prediction* | **General molecular pretraining with knowledge injection**: Uses chemical knowledge (functional groups, motifs) to guide node/edge-level masked prediction. The knowledge-guided paradigm is transferable: we can substitute polymer-specific knowledge (periodicity, monomer identity, chain position) into its framework. Also introduces graph transformer architecture relevant to our secondary GPS-style direction. |

### From arXiv (2 papers)

| # | Paper | Justification |
|---|-------|---------------|
| 3 | **MoCL** (2021) — *Data-driven Molecular Fingerprint via Knowledge-aware Contrastive Learning from Molecular Graph* | **Contrastive learning with domain knowledge**: Closest arXiv match to PerioGT-style periodicity-aware contrastive learning. Two-level knowledge integration: (a) local-level knowledge constrains augmentations to preserve graph semantics — directly applicable to polymer backbone perturbations that respect periodicity; (b) global-level knowledge encodes inter-graph similarity for richer representations. The double-contrast objective provides a template for polymer-specific contrastive pretraining. |
| 4 | **SelfGNN** (2021) — *Self-supervised Graph Neural Networks without explicit negative sampling* | **Implicit contrast for chain-like graphs**: Uses Batch Normalization to induce implicit contrastive terms, avoiding explicit negative pairs. This is structurally important for 1D polymer chains where explicit negatives are problematic — most polymers share similar backbone patterns, making hard-negative discrimination unreliable. Feature augmentation techniques (no O(N³) topological cost) are directly applicable to polymer graphs. |

### Coverage Map

| Priority Direction | Papers Addressing It |
|---|---|
| PerioGT-style contrastive learning | MoCL (domain-knowledge contrastive), SelfGNN (implicit contrast) |
| TransPolymer-style masked polymer modeling | KPGT (masked node/edge with knowledge guidance) |
| General molecular pretraining → polymers | JEPA Polymer (direct polymer SSL), all above |

> **Note on TransPolymer**: The foundational TransPolymer paper (arXiv:2209.01307) could not be fully downloaded after ~20 attempts due to a ~6MB file-size limitation in the download tool (reached 91% / 6.3MB of 6.9MB). Its masked language modeling approach for polymer sequences is well-understood from the literature and remains a key conceptual reference for Day 3-4 work.

