r descriptors for machine learning prediction of band gap and hole reorganization energy in 3120 donor-acceptor conjugated polymers, showing that radial/MOLPRINT2D fingerprints excel for band gap while frontier orbital energies are critical for reorganization energy prediction.'}, {'id': 'mem_032', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper proposes SchNet, a deep neural network architecture using continuous-filter convolutional layers to model quantum interactions in molecules, enabling joint prediction of rotationally invariant energies and energy-conserving, rotationally equivariant forces while respecting fundamental physical constraints.'}, {'id': 'mem_033', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'This paper demonstrates that graph convolutional networks (GCNs) can reliably predict polymer thermal and mechanical properties—especially glass transition temperature—with performance slightly exceeding that of extended-connectivity fingerprints, and reveals that GCN representations inherently capture backbone rigidity, a key structural determinant of polymer behavior.'}, {'id': 'mem_034', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'Using an AI-driven framework (polyVERSE) that integrates reaction-aware polymer generation, multitask graph neural networks for property prediction, and green solvent compatibility screening, the authors discover PONB-2Me5Cl—a polynorbornene dielectric with 8.3 J/cm³ energy density at 200°C, surpassing commercial polymers by 11× and enabling high-temperature electrostatic capacitors without cooling systems.'}, {'id': 'mem_035', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper demonstrates that pre-trained graph neural network (GNN) property predictors can be directly inverted via gradient ascent on constrained molecular graph representations to generate diverse molecules with targeted electronic or physicochemical properties, without any additional training.'}, {'id': 'mem_036', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper proposes Graph Convolutional Policy Network (GCPN), a reinforcement learning framework for goal-directed molecular graph generation that combines graph representation, policy gradient optimization, and adversarial training to directly optimize molecular properties while ensuring chemical validity and realism.'}, {'id': 'mem_037', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper proposes Kolmogorov–Arnold Graph Neural Networks (KA-GNNs), which fully integrate Fourier-series-based KAN modules into all core GNN components—node embedding, message passing, and readout—achieving state-of-the-art accuracy, computational efficiency, and interpretability on molecular property prediction benchmarks.'}, {'id': 'mem_038', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper introduces DimeNet, a graph neural network that uses directional message passing with rotationally equivariant message embeddings and physically inspired spherical Bessel–harmonic basis representations to explicitly model angular information, significantly outperforming prior methods on QM9 and MD17 benchmarks.'}, {'id': 'mem_039', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'TransPolymer is the first Transformer-based language model for polymer property prediction, featuring a chemically-aware tokenizer and pretraining on 5M unlabeled polymer sequences via Masked Language Modeling, achieving state-of-the-art results on ten benchmarks by effectively learning structure-property relationships from polymer sequences.'}, {'id': 'mem_040', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'EquiformerV2 improves equivariant Transformers by integrating eSCN convolutions for efficient scaling to higher-degree representations and introducing three architectural enhancements—attention re-normalization, separable S² activation, and separable layer normalization—achieving state-of-the-art accuracy on OC20 and OC22 with better speed-accuracy trade-offs and 2× reduction in DFT calculations for adsorption energy prediction.'}, {'id': 'mem_041', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'This paper proposes a data-augmented graph convolutional neural network that accurately predicts polymer glass transition temperatures from monomer structures and provides chemically interpretable insights to guide rational molecular design.'}, {'id': 'mem_042', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper proposes FGNN, a hybrid neural network that combines an enhanced graph attention network with a novel feature selection algorithm (CNMIM) to jointly leverage molecular graphs and fingerprints for improved molecular property prediction, demonstrating superior performance, noise robustness, and interpretability across multiple benchmarks.'}, {'id': 'mem_043', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper proposes Substructure-Mask Explanation (SME), a chemistry-intuitive method that explains graph neural network predictions by attributing them to chemically meaningful substructures—such as BRICS fragments, Murcko scaffolds, and functional groups—enabling reliable, interpretable, and actionable insights for molecular property prediction and structural optimization.'}, {'id': 'mem_044', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'By leveraging natural language processing to extract data from over 3,300 papers, this work demonstrates that active learning strategies could have accelerated the discovery of high-efficiency polymer solar cell donor–acceptor pairs by 75% (15 years) compared to historical trial-and-error approaches.'}, {'id': 'mem_045', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper introduces polyBERT, a Transformer-based chemical language model that treats polymer SMILES strings as a language, enabling an end-to-end, ultrafast polymer informatics pipeline that matches the accuracy of state-of-the-art handcrafted fingerprints while accelerating computation by over two orders of magnitude.'}, {'id': 'mem_046', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper introduces a Physics-Enforced Neural Network (PENN) that integrates empirical rheological equations into a neural architecture to predict polymer melt viscosity from chemistry and processing conditions, achieving superior extrapolation and physical consistency compared to physics-unaware machine learning models.'}, {'id': 'mem_047', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'This Review presents an AI-driven, application-specific protocol for designing functional and sustainable polymers by integrating target property criteria, virtual generation of synthetically accessible candidates, machine learning prediction, and experimental validation across energy and sustainability domains.'}, {'id': 'mem_048', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'Prompt-MolOpt leverages prompt engineering in a transformer architecture to enable data-efficient, multiproperty molecular optimization—even in zero-shot settings—by using property-specific embeddings to guide fragment modifications while optionally preserving user-specified pharmacophores.'}, {'id': 'mem_049', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The authors present SPACIER, an open-source framework that integrates fully automated all-atom classical molecular dynamics (via RadonPy) into Bayesian optimization pipelines for on-demand polymer design, demonstrating its capability by discovering and synthesizing optical polymers that surpass the empirical Pareto frontier between refractive index and Abbe number.'}, {'id': 'mem_050', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'This paper extends Shapley values to explain predictive uncertainty by defining information-theoretic characteristic functions based on conditional entropy and KL divergence, providing feature attributions for local uncertainty with theoretical links to conditional independence and finite-sample error control via conformal inference.'}, {'id': 'mem_051', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper proposes KANO, a knowledge graph-enhanced molecular contrastive learning framework that uses an element-oriented knowledge graph to guide both pre-training (via element-guided graph augmentation) and fine-tuning (via functional prompts), achieving state-of-the-art performance and chemically sound interpretability on 14 molecular property prediction benchmarks.'}, {'id': 'mem_052', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'This paper introduces a periodic polymer graph representation that explicitly encodes polymer chain periodicity and combines it with a message-passing neural network to automatically learn chemically relevant descriptors, achieving state-of-the-art accuracy on 8 out of 10 polymer property prediction tasks.'}, {'id': 'mem_053', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'This paper demonstrates that BigSMILES achieves polymer property prediction accuracy comparable to SMILES while enabling faster model training and lower computational costs due to its more compact textual representation, establishing it as a practical and efficient alternative for polymer machine learning tasks.'}, {'id': 'mem_054', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'This paper proposes Chem-DAGNN, a chemical-knowledge-based interpretable graph neural network that leverages small data by augmenting polyimide-specific datasets with diverse polymer glass transition temperature data, enabling the rapid discovery and experimental validation of 89 novel polyimides with exceptional heat resistance (Tg > 405°C, up to 450°C).'}, {'id': 'mem_055', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper introduces a Strongly Correlated Neural Network (SCN) that represents molecular units as adaptive neural networks and uses a self-consistent feedback loop to model strong correlations, enabling accurate forward structural prediction and one-to-many inverse design in the HP lattice protein model.'}, {'id': 'mem_056', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'This Account presents a machine learning-assisted design framework for advanced polymeric materials, addressing data scarcity and multiscale structure–property complexity through novel representations (e.g., polymer fingerprints), multifidelity modeling, and high-throughput virtual screening, thereby accelerating the discovery of high-performance polymers.'}, {'id': 'mem_057', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The authors present RadonPy, an open-source Python library that automates all-atom classical molecular dynamics simulations to compute 15 thermophysical properties for over 1000 amorphous polymers, validates results against experiments, uses transfer learning to correct systematic biases, and identifies eight polymers with high thermal conductivity (>0.4 W/m·K) along with their underlying mechanisms.'}, {'id': 'mem_058', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper introduces Joint Multi-domain Pre-training (JMP), a supervised pre-training strategy that trains a single model on 120M atomic systems from diverse chemical domains, enabling state-of-the-art performance on 34 of 40 downstream tasks and demonstrating that large-scale, multi-domain pre-training significantly improves generalization and scaling in atomic property prediction.'}, {'id': 'mem_059', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'This paper presents DMPNN-MoE, a novel deep learning model integrating directed message passing neural networks with mixture-of-experts to accurately predict temperature-dependent small-molecule solubility across diverse solute-solvent systems, achieving state-of-the-art performance and robust generalization on a large curated dataset of 56,945 experimental measurements.'}, {'id': 'mem_060', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'Uni-Mol is the first universal 3D molecular representation learning framework that pretrains SE(3)-equivariant Transformer models on 209M molecular conformations and 3M protein pockets, enabling state-of-the-art performance in both molecular property prediction and 3D spatial tasks like binding pose prediction and conformation generation.'}, {'id': 'mem_061', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper introduces M3GNet, a universal graph deep learning interatomic potential trained on 187,000+ DFT relaxation trajectories from the Materials Project, which accurately predicts energies, forces, and stresses across 89 elements and enables high-throughput discovery of stable materials.'}, {'id': 'mem_062', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'This paper introduces NequIP, an E(3)-equivariant graph neural network for interatomic potentials that achieves state-of-the-art accuracy and exceptional data efficiency by leveraging geometric tensor representations and equivariant convolutions, enabling high-fidelity molecular dynamics simulations with up to three orders of magnitude fewer training samples than existing methods.'}, {'id': 'mem_063', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper introduces a general class of material descriptors based on kernel mean embedding that preserves full distributional information of component features, enabling more accurate property prediction and guaranteed invertibility compared to conventional summary-statistic descriptors.'}, {'id': 'mem_064', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'This paper introduces a de novo design framework for solid polymer electrolytes using GPT-based and diffusion-based generative models, identifying 17 novel candidates with over 100% higher ionic conductivity than existing benchmarks through combined generative AI, graph neural network screening, and molecular dynamics validation.'}, {'id': 'mem_065', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper proposes GEM, a geometry-enhanced molecular representation learning method that integrates a dual-graph GNN architecture modeling atom-bond-angle relationships with geometry-level self-supervised learning tasks, achieving state-of-the-art performance on 14 of 15 molecular property prediction benchmarks by effectively leveraging 3D spatial structure.'}, {'id': 'mem_066', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'This paper introduces evidential deep learning for molecular property prediction, providing calibrated, single-shot epistemic uncertainty estimates without sampling, and demonstrates its utility in improving sample efficiency via active learning and boosting experimental validation rates in virtual screening.'}, {'id': 'mem_067', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'This paper presents Graphormer, a standard Transformer-based architecture enhanced with centrality, spatial, and edge encodings that effectively incorporate graph structural information, achieving state-of-the-art results on multiple graph-level prediction benchmarks including OGB-LSC.'}, {'id': 'mem_068', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'This benchmark study systematically evaluates nine uncertainty quantification methods across diverse polymer property prediction tasks, revealing that optimal UQ strategy is highly context-dependent: Ensemble excels in general prediction, BNN-MCMC in out-of-distribution scenarios, NGBoost for high-Tg polymers, and BNN-VI across diverse polymer classes.'}, {'id': 'mem_069', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper proposes eSEN, a smooth and conservative machine learning interatomic potential that passes energy conservation tests in molecular dynamics, enabling strong correlation between test errors and physical property prediction performance, and achieving state-of-the-art results in materials stability, thermal conductivity, and phonon calculations.'}, {'id': 'mem_070', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper introduces Attentive FP, a graph neural network architecture that uses a graph attention mechanism to achieve state-of-the-art predictive performance on diverse molecular property datasets while providing interpretable insights into nonlocal intramolecular interactions.'}, {'id': 'mem_071', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper introduces polyGNN, a multitask graph neural network that learns polymer features directly from repeat unit SMILES strings, achieving 1–2 orders of magnitude faster fingerprinting than handcrafted methods while maintaining comparable or better accuracy across 30+ diverse polymer properties.'}, {'id': 'mem_072', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'The paper proposes Modof, a novel deep generative model that optimizes molecules by learning to modify a single fragment at a predicted disconnection site, thereby preserving the core scaffold and better aligning with real-world medicinal chemistry practices, and demonstrates its superior performance over state-of-the-art methods on benchmark property optimization tasks.'}, {'id': 'mem_073', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'This paper analyzes the importance of node features in GNNs, demonstrating that GNN performance critically depends on the correlation between node features and node labels. For featureless graphs, the authors propose learning-based initialization methods (DeepWalk, HOPE) that construct artificial node features from graph structure, showing these synthetic features are highly competitive with—and sometimes outperform—real features across node and graph classification tasks.'}, {'id': 'mem_074', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'Presents ELENE (Edge-Level Ego-Network Encodings), a principled structural encoding that captures edge-level ego-network information—including relative degree triplets and distance signals—to boost MP-GNN expressivity, theoretically proving it can distinguish Strongly Regular Graphs (3-WL equivalent) that node-only sub-graph GNNs cannot, while achieving state-of-the-art results with up to 18.1× lower memory usage than sub-graph GNN baselines.'}, {'id': 'mem_075', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'GRIN (Graph Repetition Invariance) combines MST-aligned max-aggregation GNN with repeat-unit augmentation to learn polymer representations invariant to the number of repeating units. Theoretically, training on {1-RU, 3-RU} graphs is proven minimal for optimal repetition invariance. GRIN achieves state-of-the-art on homopolymer and copolymer property prediction, maintaining near-constant accuracy across 1x to 60x repeat-size extrapolation.'}, {'id': 'mem_076', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'GRIN (Graph Repetition INvariance) combines MST-aligned max-aggregation message passing with repeat-unit augmentation to learn polymer representations invariant to the number of repeating units, with theoretical proof that 3 RUs is the minimal augmentation for optimal invariance, achieving SOTA on 4 homopolymer and 2 copolymer property prediction benchmarks.'}, {'id': 'mem_077', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'ELENE presents edge-level ego-network encodings that capture structural signals sufficient to distinguish 3-WL equivalent Strongly Regular Graphs, with theoretical proof that Edge-Centric ELENE is strictly more expressive than Node-Centric variants, and ELENE-L—a learnable extension—matches or outperforms state-of-the-art subgraph GNNs across 10 datasets while reducing memory usage by up to 18.1×.'}, {'id': 'mem_078', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'PerioGT introduces a periodicity-aware self-supervised learning framework for polymers that uses Periodicity Augmentation (PA)-based contrastive learning, PolymerGraph with virtual nodes for auxiliary conditions, and periodicity prompt-guided fine-tuning (PGFT), achieving SOTA on 16 downstream tasks and identifying novel antimicrobial polymers in wet-lab validation.'}, {'id': 'mem_079', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'PerioGT introduces a periodicity-aware self-supervised learning framework for polymers that uses Periodicity Augmentation (PA)-based contrastive learning and periodicity prompt-guided fine-tuning to embed the inherent periodic structure of polymers into graph transformer representations, achieving SOTA on 16 downstream tasks (e.g., Tg RMSE=30.8) and experimentally identifying two potent antimicrobial polymers against MRSA.'}, {'id': 'mem_080', 'type': 'experience', 'abstract': 'Day 1 Data Centric Foundation Experiment: Tested multimer construction × node feature richness for Tg prediction with fixed GAT. Best result: 3-RU × rich features, Test RMSE=41.6K (beats baseline 50.5K by 17.6%). Feature richness strongly supported (rich >> basic, ~28K reduction). Multimer effect modest but real (3-RU > 2-RU > 1-RU). {1,3}-RU mixed underperformed pure 3-RU, contradicting GRIN hypothesis for GAT architecture.'}, {'id': 'mem_081', 'type': 'experience', 'abstract': 'Day 1 Follow-up: Tested GRIN-style max readout + L1 and ELENE edge encodings on {1,3}-RU mixed data with GAT backbone. All 5 conditions completed. Max readout consistently underperformed mean readout (~+2 RMSE). L1 sparsity (λ=1e-3) caused training instability at epoch 51 due to penalty magnitude dominating MSE loss. ELENE k=1 degree triplets added negligible value. None of the hypotheses supported: max aggregation at readout level does NOT rescue mixed augmentation for GAT.'}, {'id': 'mem_082', 'type': 'integration', 'abstract': "Feature richness dominates Tg prediction with GAT, confirming mem_073's theory that feature-label correlation is the critical determinant of GNN performance. The ~28K RMSE gap between basic and rich features dwarfs the ~2.5K multimer effect. Critically, multimer augmentation only helps when features are informative—a necessary condition: structural augmentation amplifies existing signal but cannot create signal from noise. This interaction explains why {1,3}-RU mixed training (mem_075, mem_076) underperforms pure 3-RU with GAT+mean: without GRIN's max-aggregation in message passing, mixed-scale training introduces conflicting structural signals that the readout-level mean pooling fails to resolve."}, {'id': 'mem_083', 'type': 'integration', 'abstract': "GRIN's {1,3}-RU mixed augmentation failed to transfer to GAT because GRIN's benefits depend on max-aggregation WITHIN message-passing layers, not at readout. Our follow-up experiment (mem_081) only modified the readout-level pooling, which cannot replicate the MST-aligned backbone that GRIN's max-aggregation creates during iterative message passing. Readout-level max pooling consistently underperformed mean pooling (~+2 RMSE) because it discards distributional information of atom-level embeddings critical for Tg regression. The L1 sparsity penalty (λ=1e-3) caused training destabilization when applied only to the final readout layer, highlighting that GRIN's training recipe is architecture-specific. This separates GRIN into two components: (a) data augmentation strategy and (b) architectural alignment, which must be adopted together—partial adoption can be worse than neither."}, {'id': 'mem_084', 'type': 'integration', 'abstract': "ELENE's theoretical expressivity (3-WL equivalent, 100% on SR25) did not translate to Tg prediction improvement with k=1 static encodings. This reveals a mismatch between graph isomorphism discrimination and polymer property prediction tasks: Tg depends primarily on continuous chemical features (atom identity, hybridization, aromaticity) rather than fine-grained connectivity patterns that distinguish non-isomorphic graphs. However, the null result at k=1 does not preclude ELENE's value—the paper itself shows static ELENE underperforms learnable ELENE-L, and higher k (≥2) captures chain-level connectivity patterns more relevant to polymer Tg. PerioGT's success with path/distance encodings in graph transformers (mem_078, mem_079) suggests that structural encodings for polymers should capture long-range periodic connectivity, not just local ego-network degree triplets."}, {'id': 'mem_085', 'type': 'idea', 'abstract': 'Max-Aggregation Message-Passing with Learnable k-Hop Structural Encodings for Multimer-Augmented Polymer Tg Prediction'}, {'id': 'mem_086', 'type': 'idea', 'abstract': 'Periodicity-Aware Graph Transformer with Feature-Gated Multimer Fusion and Virtual Node Global Context for Polymer Tg'}, {'id': 'mem_087', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': "SPSE replaces random walk structural encoding (RWSE) in graph transformers with simple path counts, overcoming RWSE's theoretical inability to distinguish cycles from paths; an efficient approximate counting algorithm via DAG decompositions (DFS+BFS+partial BFS) is proposed, achieving statistically significant gains on molecular and long-range graph benchmarks across GRIT, CSA, and GPS architectures."}, {'id': 'mem_088', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'GPS (Rampášek et al., 2022) proposes a modular blueprint for building scalable graph transformers by combining three ingredients: (i) positional/structural encodings categorized as local, global, or relative; (ii) a local message-passing module; and (iii) a global attention module — achieving O(N+E) linear complexity and SOTA results on 8 of 16 diverse graph benchmarks.'}, {'id': 'mem_089', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'LSPE decouples structural and positional representations in GNNs by learning both separately at every layer, using Random Walk PE initialization (k-step self-landing probabilities) to avoid Laplacian sign ambiguity, and achieving up to 64.14% improvement on molecular benchmarks.'}, {'id': 'mem_090', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'LSR-MP proposes a physics-informed framework that augments equivariant graph neural networks (EGNNs) with fragmentation-based long-range message passing, achieving up to 40% MAE reduction on MD22 and Chignolin datasets by explicitly modeling non-local interactions through bipartite atom-fragment geometric attention while alleviating over-squashing.'}, {'id': 'mem_091', 'type': 'experience', 'abstract': 'SPSE-MaxGAT experiment: SPSE edge path counts and max-aggregation both failed to improve polymer Tg prediction. Baseline improved to 36.85K via architecture scaling alone. SPSE path counts degenerate on tree-like polymer graphs. Max aggregation causes catastrophic variance collapse.'}, {'id': 'mem_092', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'TopicState updated: Day 2 SPSE-MaxGAT experiment completed. SPSE edge path counts and max-aggregation both disconfirmed. Baseline improved to 36.85K via architecture scaling. Gap to SOTA: 6.05K.'}, {'id': 'mem_093', 'type': 'experience', 'abstract': 'Architecture scaling (3→4 layers, 128→256 hidden, deeper MLP, AdamW) improved GAT+Rich+3-RU from 41.6K to 36.85K RMSE. Day 1 ceiling was undertraining, not preprocessing.'}, {'id': 'mem_094', 'type': 'experience', 'abstract': 'LSPE-GAT experiment: Node-level RWPE confirmed non-degenerate for polymers. Static RWPE k=32 achieves 35.94K — new best, breaks below 36K. LSPE decoupled learning degrades performance. Higher k helps (k=32 > k=16). SkipRWPE layer-wise injection fails.'}, {'id': 'mem_095', 'type': 'integration', 'abstract': 'Integration: Node-level RWPE works for polymers where edge-level SPSE failed. Static injection beats learned evolution for topologically simple polymer chains. Higher k needed for larger polymer graphs. Simplicity consistently beats complexity for polymer Tg prediction.'}, {'id': 'mem_096', 'type': 'integration', 'abstract': 'The Simplicity Principle for Polymer Graph Learning: Across two days of experiments, simple, well-tuned baselines consistently beat complex mechanisms imported from molecular GNN literature. This integration formalizes the pattern, identifies its domain boundaries, and surfaces implications for Day 3-4 experimental design.'}, {'id': 'mem_097', 'type': 'integration', 'abstract': 'The Topological Simplicity of Polymer Graphs as a Fundamental Design Constraint: Polymer graphs (even 3-RU) are essentially 1D chains with sparse branching. This topological simplicity explains the asymmetric success of node-level vs. edge-level encodings, the reversal of the LSPE finding, and why the scale mismatch between molecular and polymer graphs matters.'}, {'id': 'mem_098', 'type': 'integration', 'abstract': 'The Undertraining-to-True-Ceiling Trajectory: The Day 1 "preprocessing ceiling" of 41.6K RMSE was actually an undertraining artifact. Architecture scaling (+4.75K) and static RWPE (+0.91K) have reduced the gap from 10.8K to 5.14K. This integration maps the improvement trajectory, identifies what remains, and frames Day 3-4 pretraining exploration as the likely path to SOTA.'}, {'id': 'mem_099', 'type': 'idea', 'abstract': 'MoMuCon: Monomer-vs-Multimer Contrastive Pretraining on PI1M — converting the Day-1 multimer effect (3-RU > 1-RU) into a self-supervised signal that teaches the GAT encoder polymer-specific scale invariance, then finetuning on Tg'}, {'id': 'mem_100', 'type': 'idea', 'abstract': 'GPS-Lite: GAT Encoder + Single Global Attention Layer with SPD Bias — a minimal hybrid architecture that adds exactly one global attention layer after the proven GAT+RWPE encoder to capture long-range backbone periodicity without the complexity of interleaved GPS layers'}, {'id': 'mem_101', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'SelfGNN proposes a contrastive self-supervised GNN framework that avoids explicit negative sampling by leveraging Batch Normalization for implicit contrastive terms, introduces four O(1) feature augmentation techniques competitive with O(N³) topological augmentations, and achieves performance comparable to supervised GNNs while consistently outperforming semi-supervised and unsupervised baselines on node classification across seven real-world datasets.'}, {'id': 'mem_102', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'MoCL proposes knowledge-aware contrastive learning for molecular graphs, introducing bioisostere-based substructure substitution (230 rules) as local-level augmentation that preserves molecular semantics, and ECFP-based global similarity contrast to encode inter-graph structural relationships, achieving SOTA on 7 molecular benchmarks under both linear and semi-supervised evaluation.'}, {'id': 'mem_103', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'This paper applies Joint Embedding Predictive Architecture (JEPA) — a self-supervised learning method that predicts target graph embeddings from context graph embeddings in latent space rather than reconstructing input features — to stochastic polymer molecular graphs, demonstrating improved downstream property prediction in label-scarce scenarios, with particular benefit for transfer learning across different polymer chemical spaces.'}, {'id': 'mem_104', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'KPGT introduces a novel self-supervised learning framework that combines a Line Graph Transformer (LiGhT) emphasizing chemical bonds via line graph representation with path/distance encodings, and a knowledge-guided pre-training strategy that leverages molecular descriptors and fingerprints through a virtual knowledge node to guide masked node prediction, achieving state-of-the-art performance across 11 molecular property prediction benchmarks.'}, {'id': 'mem_105', 'type': 'experience', 'abstract': "Day 3 Exp#1: GraphMAE-style masked node feature pretraining on PI1M yields marginal +0.34-0.58K improvement over non-pretrained baseline — hypothesis NOT supported. Key lessons: (1) node-level masked reconstruction may be too local a pretraining task for graph-level Tg prediction, (2) pretrained models overfit more severely (4.5K val-test gap vs 1.8K baseline), (3) pretraining loss reduction (69%) confirms the task is learnable but doesn't transfer."}, {'id': 'mem_106', 'type': 'idea', 'abstract': "PSPE: Polymer-Specific Positional Encodings (distance-to-chain-end, monomer-index-along-backbone) as additive node features to improve upon static RWPE for polymer Tg prediction — testing whether explicit 1D chain-geometry signals capture what RWPE's spectral encoding only implicitly represents"}, {'id': 'mem_107', 'type': 'experience', 'abstract': "PSPE experiment: Polymer-specific positional encodings (distance-to-chain-end, monomer-index) provide modest +0.5K improvement over Day 2's RWPE-only baseline (35.44K vs 35.94K). PSPE-combined sets new best. Chain-geometry features are orthogonal to RWPE but provide only incremental gain — residual 4.64K gap to SOTA requires pretraining."}, {'id': 'mem_108', 'type': 'integration', 'abstract': 'Integration: PSPE pushes non-pretraining ceiling to 35.44K (-0.50K from Day 2). Improvement trajectory shows exponential decay (+4.75K→+0.91K→+0.50K), confirming we are asymptotically approaching the GAT+RWPE non-pretraining ceiling. Remaining 4.64K gap to SOTA likely requires pretraining. Round 1 pitfall demonstrates critical importance of reproducing known baselines before measuring experimental effects.'}, {'id': 'mem_109', 'type': 'integration', 'abstract': "Integration: The Node-to-Graph Pretraining Alignment Gap — Day 3's GraphMAE experiment (mem_105) demonstrates that node-level masked reconstruction on PI1M transfers only +0.34-0.58K to graph-level Tg prediction, despite 69% pretraining loss reduction confirming the local task is learnable. This is not a scaling failure but a structural misalignment: reconstructing atom features teaches local chemistry (valency, hybridization), while Tg depends on global polymer properties (chain stiffness, free volume, backbone flexibility) that emerge at the whole-chain level. The four Day 3 papers collectively validate this: MoCL (mem_102) uses graph-level contrastive + global similarity objectives, Polymer-JEPA (mem_103) uses embedding-space prediction at subgraph/graph level, KPGT (mem_104) succeeds with node masking only because the virtual knowledge node injects graph-level semantic descriptors, and SelfGNN (mem_101) operates at graph-level contrastive — none rely on pure node reconstruction. The alignment gap is fundamental: pretraining objectives must operate at the same structural level as the downstream task."}, {'id': 'mem_110', 'type': 'integration', 'abstract': "Integration: The Exponential Convergence to the Non-Pretraining Ceiling — Improvement trajectory across Days 1-3 shows unmistakable exponential decay: baseline 50.5K → architecture scaling +4.75K (Day 2) → static RWPE +0.91K (Day 2) → PSPE +0.50K (Day 3). The half-life of non-pretraining improvements is approximately 1 day, and the GAT+RWPE+PSPE architecture is asymptotically converging toward ~35K RMSE. The residual 4.64K gap to PerioGT's 30.8K SOTA is now clearly beyond the reach of feature engineering or GAT architecture refinement — it requires either pretraining on PI1M or a fundamentally different architectural class (graph transformers with periodicity-aware encodings). This marks the strategic pivot from non-pretraining optimization to pretraining as the primary lever."}, {'id': 'mem_111', 'type': 'integration', 'abstract': "Integration: Design Principles for Polymer Graph SSL — A synthesis of Day 3's four SSL papers (SelfGNN, MoCL, Polymer-JEPA, KPGT) and two experiments (GraphMAE negative, PSPE positive) into five actionable design principles for PI1M pretraining. (1) Graph-level objectives are mandatory — node-level reconstruction is structurally misaligned with Tg. (2) Domain-knowledge-guided augmentations dramatically outperform generic graph augmentations. (3) Embedding-space prediction (JEPA) and contrastive learning (MoCL, SelfGNN) are both viable paradigms, with JEPA offering cross-chemical-space transfer and contrastive offering simplicity. (4) Incorporating polymer-specific fingerprints/descriptors as global semantic context (KPGT-style virtual node) can bridge local pretraining tasks to global property understanding. (5) Simplicity transfers: SelfGNN's BatchNorm-only collapse prevention and Polymer-JEPA's random-walk subgraphing both demonstrate that simple mechanisms suffice when well-tuned."}, {'id': 'mem_112', 'type': 'idea', 'abstract': "SelfGNN-Polymer: Simplex Graph-Level SSL on PI1M — adapting SelfGNN's BatchNorm-only Siamese architecture with polymer-adapted Split/Standardize feature augmentations, establishing the simplest possible graph-level pretraining baseline before advancing to domain-specific SSL frameworks."}, {'id': 'mem_113', 'type': 'idea', 'abstract': "MoMuCon-Lite: SelfGNN-Style Multimer Contrastive Pretraining — replacing SelfGNN's feature augmentations with 1-RU→3-RU multimer views as the two contrastive branches, using BatchNorm-only architecture (no NT-Xent, no projection head) to test whether the multimer effect provides a stronger pretraining signal than generic feature augmentations."}, {'id': 'mem_114', 'type': 'idea', 'abstract': 'Differentiable Free Volume Head (DFVH): Physics-structured neural readout that computes Tg from GNN-predicted occupied volume and chain flexibility parameters via the Simha-Boyer free volume equation — replacing the black-box MLP predictor with a polymer-physics bottleneck, and enabling self-supervised V_occ pretraining on PI1M.'}]}

