tion) must replace generic graph augmentations. A virtual knowledge node (KPGT-style, mem_104) with RDKit polymer descriptors should supplement graph-level objectives. Simple masked node/edge prediction without graph-level semantic guidance is DEPRECATED.', 'Feature-Multimer Gating Hypothesis (Emergent): The benefit of multimer augmentation is gated by feature informativeness. Testable prediction: with rich features, multimer effect size ≈ 2.5K; with standard features, effect should be smaller but measurable; with basic features, near zero. Supported by Day 1 data but formalizable with intermediate feature richness levels.', "Simplicity-in-Supervision, Hierarchy-in-Pretraining Principle (Refined from Simplicity Principle): Simple, well-tuned baselines (rich features + architecture scaling + static node-level PEs + explicit chain-geometry encodings) consistently beat complex imported mechanisms for SUPERVISED polymer Tg learning from 7,170 labeled samples. PSPE succeeds because it exploits 1D chain geometry directly. This part remains confirmed. However, for SELF-SUPERVISED PRETRAINING on abundant unlabeled PI1M data, the complementary hierarchy principle governs: multi-level and multi-modal pretraining signals (atom-bond + fragment + graph objectives, 2D+3D modalities, domain knowledge + data-driven patterns) are complementary and incrementally beneficial, as demonstrated across all eight SSL papers (MolCHG mem_115, C-FREE mem_118, MoCL mem_102, KPGT mem_104). Simplicity and hierarchy are NOT contradictory — they apply to different regimes (limited labeled data vs. abundant unlabeled data). Every proposed innovation must still pass a 'baseline-first' gating test for supervised components.", 'Topological Simplicity Constraint (Confirmed by PSPE): Polymer graphs (even 3-RU) are fundamentally 1D tree-like chains with sparse branching. This makes edge-level alternative path counts uniformly zero and renders learned positional evolution unnecessary. Encodings must be des