| Config | Model | Test RMSE (K) |
|--------|-------|:---:|
| A | Baseline GAT (no CPA, no PSPE) | 37.45 ± 0.86 |
| B | CPA-GAT (no PSPE) | **35.52 ± 0.41** |
| C | CPA-GAT + PSPE | 35.86 ± 1.06 |

CPA delivers a strong -1.92K improvement. Adding PSPE on top yields +0.34K degradation. The features are redundant, not complementary.

### Why: CPA Subsumes PSPE

CPA's sigmoid-gated unnormalized sum preserves neighbor-count and neighbor-type-distribution signals at every layer. These cardinality signals implicitly encode chain-geometry: chain ends have fewer neighbors, monomer position correlates with neighborhood composition, backbone vs sidechain differ in degree. CPA learns what PSPE explicitly provides — the aggregation axis subsumes the chain-position feature axis.

### The Most Telling Diagnostic

Zeroing PSPE at test time causes **+55K catastrophic degradation** — the model becomes entirely dependent on PSPE yet extracts zero marginal value from it. The model latches onto PSPE as a shortcut rather than combining it synergistically with CPA.

### Theoretical Contribution

This null result establishes an **axis hierarchy** within the three-axis taxonomy: Aggregation (CPA) > Feature:Chain-Geometry (PSPE). Not all improvement axes are independent — cardinality signals capture a superset of chain-position information for 1D polymer chains.

