## PolyFNO: Concept-Leaping Beyond GNNs for Polymer Tg

I've generated and saved **mem_165** — a paradigm-shifting idea that abandons GNNs entirely and treats polymers as what they fundamentally are: **1D periodic functions**.

### The Core Leap

All prior work in this trajectory — CPA, PSPE, PolyBG, PolyWaveNet, GMT — operates within the **message-passing-on-graphs** paradigm. PolyFNO asks a more radical question: *What if the graph representation itself is the bottleneck?*

**Polymers are not arbitrary molecular graphs — they are 1D periodic chains.** Their defining feature (periodicity) makes the Fourier basis their natural representation. The FNO learns a spectral operator that:

| Fourier mode | Wavelength | Physics captured |
|:---|:---|:---|
| DC (k=0) | ∞ | Average composition (van Krevelen) |
| Low (k=1-3) | Chain-scale | Blockiness, gradient structure |
| Mid (k=4-10) | 2-10 monomers | **CRR cooperativity** (~2-4 nm) |
| High (k>10) | Bond-scale | Rotatable bonds, ring constraints |

The FNO truncates high frequencies (keeping only modes=16 out of 128), creating a **physically meaningful spectral bottleneck**: atom-level noise is filtered out while chain-scale cooperativity — the physics of Tg — is preserved.

### Why This Could Close the Irreducible Gap

PerioGT's SOTA (30.8K) uses periodicity-aware contrastive pretraining. PolyFNO achieves the same inductive bias **architecturally**: the Fourier transform of a periodic function is sparse (only harmonics of the fundamental frequency survive). This periodicity prior is built into the model rather than learned from data — potentially substituting for a significant portion of what pretraining provides.

### How It Connects to Prior Work

PolyFNO targets a completely new axis in the taxonomy: **Representational Paradigm** — upstream of aggregation, features, and architecture. If the 1D function representation is superior, CPA, GNN architectures, and message-passing innovations become irrelevant. This is the essence of a conceptual leap: not building a better GNN, but questioning whether GNNs are the right tool at all.

